# <file system> <mount point> <type> <options> <dump> <pass>
# The root is the dm-verity device the kernel builds from dm-mod.create= on its command
# line (rootfs.cmdline, written next to the image) — the only device-mapper device there
# is, so dm-0. Not LABEL=root: the filesystem is visible both through the verity device
# and, unverified, on the raw disk underneath it, so the label names two devices.
# EROFS cannot be written to at all, so `ro` is a statement rather than a choice, and
# there is no fsck pass because there is nothing a boot could have left inconsistent.
/dev/dm-0    /       erofs   ro                                0   0

# Nothing else is mounted here. /tmp is systemd's own tmp.mount and /run is PID 1's.
#
# /var is in RAM unless the VM gives it a disk, and is a unit rather than a line here:
# /usr/lib/systemd/system/var.mount (image/build-rootfs.sh) is a fallback, not a rule. A
# `systemd.mount-extra=<disk>:/var:...` on the kernel command line generates a var.mount
# of its own, which outranks /usr/lib, and the same line here would outrank both — and
# could not be taken back by a VM that has no disk. The image ships /var empty; what has
# to be in it is created at boot by systemd-tmpfiles (and StateDirectory=).
#
# /home and /root are symlinks to var/home and var/roothome, as on ostree systems, so they
# are RAM or disk with /var and need no line of their own.
